From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Bradburne <abradburne@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 09:02:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] blk: stop the partition scan at the first unused entry

part_create_block_devices() tried all MAX_SEARCH_PARTITIONS (128)
entries, and every part_get_info() reads and checks the whole GPT again:
for the RG DS+ card's 12 partitions the 116 empty entries cost 0.15 s of
every boot.

Stop at the first entry that isn't there. Only EFI uses these partition
devices; a table with holes in its numbering would lose the partitions
after the first hole.

Upstream status: board-specific hack, not for upstream as written (holes
in a GPT are legal); a cached GPT read would be the general fix.
---
 drivers/block/blk-uclass.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/block/blk-uclass.c b/drivers/block/blk-uclass.c
index 73c24fd9..eb6bf034 100644
--- a/drivers/block/blk-uclass.c
+++ b/drivers/block/blk-uclass.c
@@ -796,8 +796,13 @@ static int part_create_block_devices(struct udevice *blk_dev)
 
 	/* Add devices for each partition */
 	for (count = 0, part = 1; part <= MAX_SEARCH_PARTITIONS; part++) {
+		/*
+		 * Stop at the first unused entry: every part_get_info() reads and
+		 * checks the whole GPT again, and the 116 empty entries of a
+		 * 12-partition card took 0.15 s. Only EFI uses these devices.
+		 */
 		if (part_get_info(desc, part, &info))
-			continue;
+			break;
 		snprintf(devname, sizeof(devname), "%s:%d", blk_dev->name,
 			 part);
 
